  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nAbove normal highs continue into the Long Term period as upper-level \nridging builds overhead and moves eastward. Afternoon highs in the \n90s can be expected for most early next week, with cooler \ntemperatures in the 80s across portions of the higher terrain and \nsoutheast New Mexico. Meanwhile, overnight lows in the 60s to low \n70s are forecast through Tuesday night. By early Tuesday next week, \nmodel guidance shows a change in the weather pattern as an upper-\nlevel trough approaches our region from the west. Remnant moisture \nfrom what is now Hurricane Simon gets pulled into the aforementioned \nsystem early next week. This yields low (10-30%) rain chances across \nportions of our westernmost zones and southeast New Mexico Monday \nnight. Increasing moisture and lift from the trough bring higher \nrain chances (20-50%) Tuesday through Friday next week. At this \ntime, the best rain chances look to be Thursday and Friday as a cold \nfront pushes through, providing additional lift. Another hazard we \nmay need to monitor for is the possibility of high winds in the \nGuadalupe/Delaware Mountains. Though this will depend on the track \nof the system mid-to-late next week. Naturally, we will continue to \nmonitor trends and adjust the forecast as needed. Otherwise, \nensemble guidance indicates a cooling trend during the latter half \nof next week due to lower heights associated with the trough/cold \nfront.\n  \nGreening",
